Here are 80/20, low‑touch fixes you can drop in to clear the majority of errors.

1) Replace Bun.HTMLRewriter with a tiny streaming-free parser
Goal: strip tags and ignore script/style/noscript. htmlparser2 is the lightest widely-used option and mirrors your callback style without bringing in a DOM.

Non‑streaming (simplest; good for CLI-sized pages):
```ts
import { Parser } from "htmlparser2";

export function htmlToText(html: string): string {
  let text = "";
  let skip = 0;
  const skipTags = new Set(["script", "style", "noscript"]);

  const parser = new Parser(
    {
      onopentag(name) { if (skipTags.has(name)) skip++; },
      ontext(data) { if (skip === 0) text += data; },
      onclosetag(name) { if (skipTags.has(name)) skip--; },
    },
    { decodeEntities: true }
  );

  parser.end(html);
  return text;
}
```

If you truly need streaming later, htmlparser2 also exports a WritableStream; you can feed chunks from fetch(Response).body via Readable.fromWeb(), but the non‑streaming version is the 80/20 fix.

2) NamedError + z.void(): allow zero-arg constructor only for void schemas
Change the constructor to use conditional tuple types. You fix it once in NamedError.create; all call sites like new UI.CancelledError() start working, while other error classes still require data.

```ts
import { z, ZodTypeAny, ZodVoid } from "zod";

type ErrorCtorArgs<D extends ZodTypeAny> =
  D extends ZodVoid ? [options?: ErrorOptions] : [data: z.input<D>, options?: ErrorOptions];

export const NamedError = {
  create<Name extends string, D extends ZodTypeAny>(name: Name, schema: D) {
    const isVoid = schema instanceof ZodVoid;
    return class extends Error {
      readonly name = name;
      readonly data: z.output<D>;
      constructor(...args: ErrorCtorArgs<D>) {
        const [a0, options] = args as [unknown, ErrorOptions?];
        super(name, options);
        // For void schemas, no data; otherwise capture data
        this.data = (isVoid ? undefined : a0) as z.output<D>;
      }
    };
  },
};

// Usage
export const CancelledError = NamedError.create("UICancelledError", z.void());
// OK:
throw new CancelledError();
```

Notes:
- Keep data typed as z.output<D>; for z.void() that’s void (assignable from undefined).
- No broadening of other error constructors; only z.void() becomes zero‑arg.

3) JSON.parse returns unknown: centralize the assertion in one place
Do the unsafe cast once inside readJSON<T>() so all call sites regain types. Optionally add an overload that accepts a Zod schema for the few critical reads you want runtime validation on.

```ts
import { promises as fs } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import { z, ZodTypeAny } from "zod";

export async function readJSON<T>(key: string): Promise<T>;
export async function readJSON<S extends ZodTypeAny>(
  key: string,
  schema: S
): Promise<z.infer<S>>;
export async function readJSON(
  key: string,
  schema?: ZodTypeAny
): Promise<unknown> {
  const t = await fs.readFile(path.join(dir, key + ".json"), "utf8");
  const parsed = JSON.parse(t);
  return schema ? schema.parse(parsed) : (parsed as unknown as never);
}

// Example (no code changes at call sites)
const info = await Storage.readJSON<MessageV2.Info>(p); // now typed

// Optional guarded read
const info2 = await Storage.readJSON(p, MessageV2.InfoSchema); // parsed+typed
```

This clears the unknown cascade (~71 errors) without touching call sites. Over time, you can migrate hot paths to the schema overload.

4) “Property X does not exist on type {}” with zod-inferred objects: common causes and quick fixes
It’s rarely a tsconfig/moduleResolution issue. In practice, {} leaks in via inference or schema widening. The 80/20 checklist:

- Don’t widen your schema to ZodTypeAny.
  - Problem:
    const CfgSchema: z.ZodTypeAny = z.object({ experimental: ... });
    type Cfg = z.infer<typeof CfgSchema>; // loses structure downstream
  - Fix:
    export const CfgSchema = z.object({ experimental: z.object({ hook: z.object({ file_edited: z.function() }) }) });
    export type Cfg = z.infer<typeof CfgSchema>;

- Make sure your value is annotated at the parse/read boundary so generics don’t infer {}.
  - Good:
    const cfg: Cfg = await Storage.readJSON<Cfg>("config");
    // or, if validating:
    const cfg = CfgSchema.parse(await Storage.readJSON("config"));
  - Bad (lets T be inferred as {} in many helpers):
    const cfg = await Storage.readJSON("config"); // cfg: unknown -> flows into helpers -> {} after Partial/merge

- Generic helpers that default to {}, or take unknown, will collapse shapes.
  - Problem:
    function merge<T extends object = {}>(a: T, b: Partial<T>): T { ... } // T often infers as {}
  - Fix:
    function merge<T extends Record<string, unknown>>(a: T, b: Partial<T>): T { ... }
    // Even better: anchor inference on the first arg and avoid a default:
    function merge<T extends Record<string, unknown>>(a: T, b: Partial<T>): T { ... }

- Partial/Required on unknown produces {}-like effects through unions/optionals.
  - If you have utilities returning Partial<T> but T is inferred as unknown, the result becomes {}.
  - Anchor T at the call site or switch the return to the concrete alias (Cfg) when you know it.

- z.object({}) vs passthrough: if a nested object is declared as z.object({}) (strict), TypeScript literally thinks it has no keys.
  - If you intentionally allow unknown keys, use .passthrough() or .catchall(z.unknown()).
  - If keys exist, define them in the schema so hook/file_edited are known.

Putting it together: most {} errors should disappear once readJSON<T>() returns typed T again and any schema variables are not widened to ZodTypeAny. The remaining instances usually trace to one or two generic utilities defaulting to {} — remove that default and/or annotate the first argument’s type so T is anchored.

Summary of minimal changes to fix the bulk
- HTML→text: switch to htmlparser2 with a 20‑line helper (no DOM, no streaming needed).
- Errors with z.void(): update NamedError.create to use conditional tuple args; zero‑arg constructor works only for void schemas.
- JSON parsing: one centralized cast in readJSON<T>(), plus optional schema overload for gradual hardening.
- {} property errors: stop schema widening; return typed cfg at the boundary; remove generic defaults of {}; use passthrough/catchall or declare real keys.

If you want, paste your NamedError.create and one of the generic helpers producing cfg so I can adapt the exact types with your code.
